" **Fleet Spares Procurement Policy**\n\n1. **Approval Thresholds and Authorization**\n   - Purchases up to $5,000 can be made by the Fleet Spares Manager.\n   - Purchases between $5,000 and $20,000 require approval from the Procurement Officer.\n   - Purchases over $20,000 must be approved by the Finance Director.\n\n2. **Quotation Requirements**\n   - For purchases under $5,000, a single quote is required.\n   - For purchases between $5,000 and $20,000, three quotes are required.\n   - For purchases over $20,000, five quotes are required.\n\n3. **Preferred-Supplier Rule**\n   - Preferred suppliers, who have demonstrated consistency in quality, delivery, and pricing, should be utilized whenever possible. However, exceptions can be made if alternative suppliers offer more competitive rates or better terms.\n\n4. **Emergency Purchase Procedure**\n   - In case of emergencies where a vessel is at sea or about to sail, the Fleet Spares Manager may authorize immediate purchases up to $10,000 without the usual quotation process. The procurement officer must be notified immediately after such purchases.\n   - For emergency purchases over $10,000, the Finance Director's approval is required, and the usual quotation process should be followed as soon as possible.\n\n5. **Maximum Budget Variance**\n   - Any variance in the budget for fleet spares exceeding 10% requires re-approval from the same authority who initially approved the purchase.\n\n6. **Stock-Level Rule for Critical Spares**\n   - Minimum stock levels for critical spares should be maintained as follows:\n     - Engine parts: 2 months' supply\n     - Navigation and communication equipment: 1 month's supply\n     - Electrical components: 1.5 months' supply\n     - Safety equipment: Always fully stocked\n\n7. **Inventory Management**\n   - The Inventory Manager is responsible for maintaining accurate records of all spares and ensuring that stock levels are regularly reviewed and adjusted as needed.\n\n8. **Supplier Performance Evaluation**\n   - Suppliers will be evaluated annually based on factors such as delivery times, quality of goods, responsiveness to requests, and pricing competitiveness. Poor performing suppliers may be removed from the preferred supplier list.\n\n9. **Conflict Resolution**\n   - In case of disputes or disagreements regarding procurement decisions, the matter will be escalated to the Procurement Committee consisting of the Procurement Officer, Finance Director, and Fleet Spares Manager. The committee's decision will be final.\n\n10. **Policy Review**\n    - This policy will be reviewed annually by the Procurement Committee to ensure it remains effective and relevant to Meridian Marine Ltd's needs. Suggestions for improvements are welcome from all employees."